#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3a71018-8f67-53cb-aa6c-7f17e0af9ef9" :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by the Number of Terms It Has"}] :question/id #uuid "b8742776-1dc7-5816-afb6-9a72142ecb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10650" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $2y^2+3$ based on the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $2y^2+3$ has two terms, so it is a binomial."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "6270e77f-6ccb-5304-90da-2419fa309007" :question/math-academy-id "e-10651" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $1+2x^2 - 8x$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $1+2x^2 - 8x$ has three terms, so it is a trinomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $2,$ so it is quadratic. \n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quadratic trinomial."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/id #uuid "3400928d-9463-56f2-af47-e193a1e18a47" :question/math-academy-id "e-10652" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The sum of $x^2+2$ and $3x$ is a trinomial.\n\nII. The sum of $5x^2+4x$ and $-4x$ is a trinomial.\n\nIII. The sum of a monomial and a binomial is **always** a trinomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n(x^2+2) + (3x) = x^2+3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n(5x^2+4x) + (-4x) = 5x^2,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a trinomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of a monomial and a binomial *could be* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement I).\n\nHowever, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial (see, for example, the statement II).\n\nSo, the sum of a monomial and a binomial is *not always* a trinomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b8feb96-7432-5fe7-a6c7-b2d8ebe7e766" :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by Its Degree and Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa48fab-715e-5ede-99d8-5d064c0e6739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cubic monomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475619} {:answer/id #uuid "7e58282b-3e4e-5722-ac04-ace8c4d48e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "quartic trinomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475629} {:answer/id #uuid "b15c50f0-be03-5370-b810-2fe0777248de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "quartic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475703} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "quartic monomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475722} {:answer/id #uuid "edff33b5-ad53-5c02-89fa-091a3ea8308e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "cubic binomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9e3a7dd-30c5-5a0e-ad80-71e2058da5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "quartic monomial" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475722} :answer-field/id #uuid "5117de3a-07aa-5519-87b1-38042bdf315a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e00081d5-c6bc-5ebe-9c4d-19fd46daf244" :question/math-academy-id "q-111225" :question/problem "Categorize the polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ based on its degree and the number of terms it has." :question/worked-solution "The polynomial $3{h}^{4}$ has only one term, so it is a monomial.\n\nAlso, its degree is $4,$ so it is a quartic.\n\nTherefore, the polynomial is a quartic monomial."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "031cfe8a-aedb-5c47-85b8-b78d424c9253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475450} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb13528-7dc2-5e8c-81d3-76237471c1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475557} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2bd94-3507-5818-a8e2-21b4e3d5daaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475630} {:answer/id #uuid "8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475658} {:answer/id #uuid "c9db9c56-7c9d-5fbd-9ab7-859523a2dbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8efd74f5-cafb-5856-a477-190813a8a4ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475658} :answer-field/id #uuid "68adcac4-f714-5f55-81da-9c003d947562" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c70515f-461b-5406-a00b-651a86f2659d" :question/math-academy-id "q-152252" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nII. ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is cubic trinomial.\n\nIII. **Some** trinomials are cubic." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the degree of the trinomial $5{x}^{2}-7x+1$ is $2,$ it is a quadratic trinomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the degree of the trinomial ${x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x$ is $3,$ it is a cubic trinomial.\n\n- Statement III is true. A trinomial *could be* cubic (see, for example, statement II). \n\n However, a trinomial is *not always* cubic (see, for example, statement I). \n\n So, some trinomials are cubic.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475925 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054712 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd2d5a4-73de-51ad-9f9f-070d1d4f31a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae009f5-4ad8-554c-9afe-f7df0d60df6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475460} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475577} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d1b1b3-57ce-5b6f-aeae-962b95ce3397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475768} {:answer/id #uuid "e11a5ec7-6258-52b7-931d-7cf5782a0e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475786} {:answer/id #uuid "ec85cf8b-2ad9-57c7-abee-ddf2ba3c9e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb50a14-8d14-5abf-bbba-8ffff9b40b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475577} :answer-field/id #uuid "62c88c7f-5c96-5f04-a40f-dca9bee2c854" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "298a3ab5-9f3b-5907-82bb-2239de27222c" :question/math-academy-id "q-152438" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The product of $7x$ and $3{x}^{2}$ is a binomial.\n\nII. The product of $4x$ and $5$ is a monomial.\n\nIII. The product of two monomials is **always** a binomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n(7x)\\times (3{x}^{2})=21{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a binomial (it's a monomial).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n(4x)\\times (5)=20x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a monomial.\n\n- Statement III is false. There are products of two monomials that are not binomials (see, for example, statement I and II).\n\nIn fact, the product of two monomials is *never* a binomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186475965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054710 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3a71018-8f67-53cb-aa6c-7f17e0af9ef9" :knowledge-point/title "Categorizing a Polynomial by the Number of Terms It 